-0.00962066650390625, -0.03594970703125, -0.01160430908203125, -0.04290771484375, -0.006847381591796875, 0.03350830078125, -0.006500244140625, -0.0247344970703125, 0.00792694091796875, -0.0011339187622070312, 0.0199737548828125, 0.01439666748046875, -0.04168701171875, -0.01406097412109375, -0.010009765625, 0.00890350341796875, 0.00930023193359375, 0.005779266357421875, 0.010986328125, -0.0269775390625, 0.0102386474609375, -0.0013761520385742188, -0.01329803466796875, 0.0012645721435546875, -0.007541656494140625, -0.048309326171875, 0.021453857421875, 0.0125579833984375, -0.0172882080078125, 0.0047760009765625, 0.00978851318359375, -0.005859375, 0.0200958251953125, -0.00266265869140625, -0.01387786865234375, -0.0255126953125, 0.021270751953125, 0.0103759765625, -0.0190277099609375, -0.002353668212890625, 0.0085906982421875, -0.0303192138671875, 0.0240936279296875, 0.004085540771484375, 0.00406646728515625, 0.012786865234375, -0.0016565322875976562, 0.018402099609375, 0.00153350830078125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**Introduction and Motivation**\n\nThe emergence of organized spatiotemporal patterns is ubiquitous in oscillatory systems, from neural populations to engineered networks. However, existing methods\u2014such as global order parameters or traditional phase coherence measures\u2014often struggle to capture transient dynamics or to distinguish between different spatial organizations when global synchronization levels are similar. Specifically, these methods may (1) conflate distinct spatial patterns such as global synchrony and traveling waves due to similar synchronization indices, (2) miss short-lived or spatially localized patterns because of reliance on time-averaged metrics, and (3) lack sensitivity to spatial arrangement, failing to detect local clusters or propagating waves (see e.g., Breakspear et al., 2010; Pikovsky et al., 2001). Recent local or pattern-based metrics, such as local phase synchrony indices or cluster-based approaches (e.g., Rubido et al., 2014; M\u00fcller et al., 2016), provide improved spatial sensitivity but often require a priori cluster definitions or are less suited to capturing transient, evolving patterns. Identifying and tracking these patterns as they evolve remains a significant challenge, particularly in the presence of complex or short-lived dynamics.\n\nHere, we specifically address the research question: How can we robustly and efficiently detect and quantify transient and spatially organized phase patterns in oscillatory networks, even when global synchronization metrics are similar? Our proposed method aims to fill this gap by providing a local, pattern-based approach that is sensitive to both spatial arrangement and temporal evolution, and that generalizes to arbitrary network structures without requiring predefined clusters.\n\n**Spatial Ordinal Pattern Framework**\n\n*Overview and Motivation*\n\nWe introduce a framework based on spatial ordinal patterns to characterize the spatiotemporal dynamics of oscillatory systems. In this approach, we focus on the phase relationships between neighboring oscillators, rather than their amplitudes. Specifically, for each oscillator, we compare its phase to those of its neighbors and encode the local ordering as a symbolic pattern.\n\n*Step-by-Step Example of Symbolic Encoding*\n\nTo clarify the encoding process, consider a one-dimensional chain of three oscillators with phases \u03b8\u2081, \u03b8\u2082, \u03b8\u2083. We compare \u03b8\u2081 to \u03b8\u2082 and \u03b8\u2082 to \u03b8\u2083:\n\n- If \u03b8\u2081 < \u03b8\u2082 and \u03b8\u2082 \u2248 \u03b8\u2083 (where \"\u2248\" denotes a phase difference below a threshold), the pattern is encoded as [\u2018<\u2019, \u2018=\u2019].\n- Ties are represented by a special symbol (\u2018=\u2019) when the phase difference between an oscillator and its neighbor falls below a threshold.\n\nFor higher-dimensional neighborhoods or more neighbors, ties are assigned between any pair of neighbors whose phase differences are within the threshold, resulting in patterns that may include multiple tie symbols. For example, consider a two-dimensional 3\u00d73 grid neighborhood centered on oscillator \u03b8\u2085, with neighbors \u03b8\u2081\u2013\u03b8\u2084 and \u03b8\u2086\u2013\u03b8\u2089. Suppose \u03b8\u2085 \u2248 \u03b8\u2081 < \u03b8\u2082 \u2248 \u03b8\u2083 < \u03b8\u2084, where \u03b8\u2085 and \u03b8\u2081 are nearly equal, \u03b8\u2082 and \u03b8\u2083 are nearly equal, and both pairs are less than \u03b8\u2084. The pattern could be encoded as [\u2018=\u2019, \u2018<\u2019, \u2018=\u2019, \u2018<\u2019], reflecting both ties and orderings among neighbors. Figure 1 in the main text provides a schematic illustration of this encoding process.\n\n*Threshold Selection for \"Nearly Equal\" Phases*\n\nA threshold for \"nearly equal\" phases is set based on a small fixed value (e.g., a fraction of \u03c0), chosen to be robust to noise yet sensitive to meaningful differences. In practice, this threshold can be selected using empirical heuristics, sensitivity analysis, or cross-validation on labeled data. For sensitivity analysis, we systematically vary the threshold and assess the stability of detected patterns and entropy values; for cross-validation, we select the threshold that maximizes discrimination between known regimes (e.g., synchrony vs. waves) in labeled synthetic or experimental data. The range 0.1\u03c0\u20130.2\u03c0 was determined empirically by running simulations on synthetic networks and analyzing labeled EEG data, where we observed that within this range, entropy values and pattern detection remained stable, while thresholds outside this range either became too sensitive to noise (for smaller values) or failed to distinguish meaningful patterns (for larger values). This is illustrated in Fig. 2b and summarized in Supplementary Table 1. This ensures that both phase gradients and synchronized clusters are captured within a single framework.\n\n*Algorithmic Steps*\n\nThe main algorithm proceeds as follows:\n1. For each oscillator at each time point, identify its neighborhood (e.g., adjacent nodes in a lattice or network).\n2. Compute the phase differences between the oscillator and each neighbor.\n3. Assign symbolic relations (\u2018<\u2019, \u2018=\u2019, \u2018>\u2019) based on the threshold for \"nearly equal\" phases.\n4. Encode the resulting sequence of symbols as the local spatial ordinal pattern.\n5. Aggregate the patterns across all oscillators to estimate the spatial distribution of patterns at each time point.\n6. Compute the spatial permutation entropy by evaluating the probability distribution of these patterns across the network.\n\nA flowchart summarizing these steps is provided in Fig. 1.\n\n**Spatial Permutation Entropy**\n\n*Definition and Normalization*\n\nFrom this symbolic representation, we define a spatial permutation entropy, which quantifies the diversity of spatial ordinal patterns present at each time point. This entropy is computed by evaluating the probability distribution of the observed patterns across the network at each time step. To facilitate comparison across different network sizes or pattern lengths, the entropy is normalized by the logarithm of the total number of possible patterns (including tie symbols) for the given neighborhood size.\n\n*Intuitive Explanation of Pattern Counting*\n\nFor a neighborhood of size n, the number of possible patterns is given by the number of weak orderings (permutations with ties), which can be calculated as the sum over k = 1 to n of S(n, k) \u00d7 k!, where S(n, k) is the Stirling number of the second kind. Intuitively, this counts all possible ways to order n items, allowing for ties; for example, with n = 3, there are 13 possible patterns: 6 strict orderings (no ties), 6 with one tie, and 1 with all equal. For further details on weak orderings and Stirling numbers, see Stanley (2012). Supplementary Table 1 provides explicit values for other n.\n\nThe probability distribution is estimated over the spatial ensemble of oscillators at each time point, rather than over a temporal window, allowing for the detection of rapid changes. High entropy indicates a wide variety of local phase configurations, while low entropy reflects more uniform or ordered states. This enables the detection of transient dynamics and regime transitions as they occur.\n\n**Validation and Results**\n\n*Distinguishing Spatial Patterns and Effect Sizes*\n\nWe demonstrate that our approach can distinguish between phase-locked states that have identical levels of global synchronization but differ in their spatial organization, such as uniform synchrony versus traveling waves. In practice, uniform synchrony yields a dominance of a single ordinal pattern (all phases equal or nearly equal), resulting in low entropy, while traveling waves produce a consistent gradient in phase across space, leading to a different dominant pattern or a broader distribution of patterns. Additionally, the method effectively characterizes partially synchronized states, where clusters of oscillators are synchronized locally but not globally.\n\nQuantitatively, in synthetic networks (100 simulations per regime), spatial permutation entropy increased by up to 60% (mean increase 0.21, SD 0.05, p < 0.01, paired t-test; p-values uncorrected as only two regimes were compared per test) when transitioning from uniform synchrony to traveling wave regimes. Transient events were detected within a few time steps of their onset, with detection latency below 5% of the characteristic oscillation period (see Fig. 2 for representative time courses and statistical summaries; confidence intervals are provided in Supplementary Table 2). These effect sizes indicate that the method is not only statistically significant but also practically sensitive to meaningful changes in spatial organization.\n\n*Application to EEG Data*\n\nWe validate the method on synthetic oscillator networks, including both regular lattice and random network topologies, across multiple spatiotemporal regimes. Furthermore, we apply the framework to resting-state EEG recordings from human volunteers (n = 12), recorded during alternating eyes-open and eyes-closed conditions. EEG data were preprocessed using standard artifact rejection: independent component analysis (ICA) was used to remove ocular and muscle artifacts, with components identified for removal based on their time courses and topographies (criteria: stereotyped artifact patterns and correlation with EOG/EMG channels). Channels with excessive noise or flat signals (variance below 1 \u03bcV\u00b2 or above 100 \u03bcV\u00b2) were excluded (mean 1.2 channels per subject), and epochs with residual artifacts (amplitude exceeding \u00b1100 \u03bcV) were rejected. Data were then bandpass filtered (8\u201313 Hz, alpha band, chosen for its relevance to resting-state dynamics) and re-referenced to the average reference. Task labels were assigned based on experimental timing logs marking eyes-open and eyes-closed periods. Cognitive or behavioral conditions were distinguished based on experimental task labels and changes in the spatial permutation entropy profiles within individual subjects. For example, entropy values were consistently higher during eyes-open compared to eyes-closed periods (mean difference 0.12, 95% CI [0.08, 0.16], p < 0.005, Wilcoxon signed-rank test, n = 12 subjects), reflecting increased spatial diversity of phase patterns (see Fig. 3). While these results are promising, the relatively small sample size (n = 12) limits the generalizability of the findings; larger cohorts and replication studies will be important for confirming robustness across populations.\n\n*Computational Requirements*\n\nThe method scales linearly with the number of oscillators and neighborhood size."}}